  "aiOverview": {
    "content": "High cholesterol is a waxy substance in the blood, often caused by poor diet (saturated/trans fats), lack of exercise, or genetics, which can cause arterial plaque buildup. Typically, high cholesterol has no symptoms, necessitating blood tests (lipid panels). It is managed by reducing saturated fats, exercising, and medications. Key Aspects of Cholesterol Management: Main Causes: High intake of saturated and trans fats, obesity, sedentary lifestyle, smoking, and genetics. Symptoms: Generally none; it is a \"silent\" condition, often discovered via testing or when a heart attack or stroke occurs. How to Lower It: Diet: Consume foods low in saturated fat, such as fruits, vegetables, whole grains, and foods high in fiber. Activity: Exercise regularly to increase \"good\" (HDL) cholesterol. Lifestyle: Avoid smoking and manage weight. Medication: Doctors may prescribe medications, such as statins, to manage levels. Foods to Avoid/Limit: Red meat, full-fat dairy products (butter, cheese), baked goods, fried foods, and tropical oils (coconut/palm oil). Risks: High levels of LDL (\"bad\") cholesterol, known as hypercholesterolemia, increase the risk of plaque build-up in arteries (atherosclerosis), leading to heart disease and stroke.",
